  143  p8  heading (A)  [p8-l1]  A Proof of Lem. 1
  144  p8  paragraph  [p8-l2..p8-l3 (2)]  Lemma 1. Given a tight, trim PCFG in CNF and a string w = w1 · · · wN, the prefix probability of a\nsubstring wi+1 · · · wk of w being derived from X can be defined recursively as follows:
  145  p8  formula (10)  [p8-l7..p8-l8 (7)]  k−1\nπ(i, X, k) = X ξ(Y Z | X) · X β(i, Y, j) · π(j, Z, k) (10)\nY,Z∈N j=i+1
  146  p8  paragraph  [p8-l11]  Base case (for all k ∈ 0 . . . N − 1 and all X ∈ N):
  147  p8  formula (11)  [p8-l12..p8-l13 (4)]  π(k, X, k+1) = X ξ(Y | X) · p(Y−→wk+1) (11)\nY∈N
  148  p8  paragraph  [p8-l16..p8-l19 (4)]  Proof. Eq. (11): The base case, π(k, X, k+1), is the probability of wk+1 being the left-most terminal\nin the parse subtree under X. It is, therefore, simply the sum of probabilities of any non-terminal Y being\non the left corner of the parse subtree under X multiplied by the corresponding probability of Y directly\nderiving wk+1.
  149  p8  paragraph  [p8-l20..p8-l22 (3)]  Eq. (10): Given the PCFG is in CNF and assuming k > i + 1, in order to derive the prefix wi+1 · · · wk\nwe must first apply some rule X −→ Y Z, where the first part of the substring is then derived from Y and\nthe remainder (and potentially more) from Z:
  150  p8  formula (17)  [p8-l27..p8-l28 (8)]  k−1\nπ(i, X, k) = X p(X −→ Y Z) X β(i, Y, j) · π(j, Z, k) + π(i, Y, k) (17)\nY,Z∈N j=i+1
  151  p8  paragraph  [p8-l31..p8-l36 (6)]  where the last term, π(i, Y, k), handles the case where the whole prefix is derived from Y alone.\nThis term is clearly recursively defined through Eq. (17), with X replaced by Y. Defining\nR(Y, Z) def= Pk−1j=i+1 β(i, Y, j)π(j, Z, k), we can rewrite Eq. (17) as:
  152  p8  formula (18)  [p8-l37..p8-l40 (6)]  π(i, X, k) = X p(X −→ Y Z) · R(Y, Z) + X p(X −→ A B) · π(i, A, k) (18)\nY,Z∈N A,B∈N
  153  p8  paragraph  [p8-l43]  After repeated substitutions ad infinitum, we get:
  154  p8  formula (19)  [p8-l44..p8-l47 (6)]  π(i, X, k) = X p(X ∗⇒ A B) X p(A −→ Y Z) · R(Y, Z) (19)\nA,B∈N Y,Z∈N
  155  p8  paragraph  [p8-l50..p8-l51 (2)]  Note that, in the last step, infinite derivations do not carry any probability mass since we assumed the\nPCFG to be tight and trim. Hence, the final form of the equation is:
  156  p8  formula (20)  [p8-l52..p8-l61 (12)]  π(i, X, k) = X p(X ∗⇒ A B) X p(A −→ Y Z) · R(Y, Z)\nA,B∈N Y,Z∈N\nk−1 (20)\n= X ξ(Y Z | X) X β(i, Y, j) · π(j, Z, k)\nY,Z∈N j=i+1
  157  p8  paragraph  [p8-l64]  ■
  158  p8  furniture/page-number  [p8-l65]  64
  159  p9  heading (B)  [p9-l1]  B Extension of Alg. 3 to Semirings
  160  p9  paragraph  [p9-l2..p9-l3 (2)]  In the following, we give the necessary background on semirings and then show how the algorithms\nintroduced above can be framed in terms of semirings.
  161  p9  heading (B.1)  [p9-l4..p9-l5 (2)]  B.1 Semirings
  162  p9  paragraph  [p9-l6]  We start by introducing the necessary definitions and notation.
  163  p9  paragraph  [p9-l7]  Definition 10. A monoid is a 3-tuple ⟨A, ◦, 1⟩ where:
  164  p9  list_item ((i))  [p9-l8]  (i) A is a non-empty set;
  165  p9  list_item ((ii))  [p9-l9]  (ii) ◦ is an associative binary operation: ∀a, b, c ∈ A,(a ◦ b) ◦ c = a ◦ (b ◦ c);
  166  p9  list_item ((iii))  [p9-l10]  (iii) 1 is a left and right identity element: ∀a ∈ A, 1 ◦ a = a ◦ 1 = a
  167  p9  list_item ((iv))  [p9-l11]  (iv) A is closed under the operation ◦: ∀a, b ∈ A, a ◦ b ∈ A
  168  p9  paragraph  [p9-l12]  A monoid is commutative if ∀a, b ∈ A : a ◦ b = b ◦ a.
  169  p9  paragraph  [p9-l13]  Definition 11. A semiring is a 5-tuple W = ⟨A, ⊕, ⊗, 0, 1⟩, where
  170  p9  list_item ((i))  [p9-l14]  (i) ⟨A, ⊕, 0⟩ is a commutative monoid over A with identity element 0 under the addition operation ⊕;
  171  p9  list_item ((ii))  [p9-l15]  (ii) ⟨A, ⊗, 1⟩ is a monoid over A with identity element 1 under the multiplication operation ⊗;
  172  p9  list_item ((iii))  [p9-l16]  (iii) Multiplication is distributive over addition, that is, ∀a, b, c ∈ A:
  173  p9  list_item (•)  [p9-l17]  • a ⊗ (b ⊕ c) = a ⊗ b ⊕ a ⊗ c;
  174  p9  list_item (•)  [p9-l18]  • (b ⊕ c) ⊗ a = b ⊗ a ⊕ c ⊗ a.
  175  p9  list_item ((iv))  [p9-l19]  (iv) 0 is an annihilator for A, that is, ∀a ∈ A, 0 ⊗ a = a ⊗ 0 = 0.
  176  p9  paragraph  [p9-l20..p9-l21 (2)]  A semiring is commutative if ⟨A, ⊗, 1⟩ is a commutative monoid. A semiring is idempotent if ∀a ∈ A :\na ⊕ a = a.
  177  p9  paragraph  [p9-l22..p9-l25 (4)]  Definition 12. A semiring W = ⟨A, ⊕, ⊗, 0, 1⟩ is complete if it is possible to extend the addition operator\n⊕ to infinite sums, maintaining the properties of associativity, commutativity, and distributivity from the\nfinite case (Rozenberg and Salomaa, 1997, Chapter 9). In this case, we can define the unary operation of\nthe Kleene star denoted by a superscript ∗ as the infinite sum over powers of its operand, that is, ∀a ∈ A:
  178  p9  formula (21)  [p9-l28..p9-l29 (6)]  ∞\na∗ def= M ai (21)\ni=0
  179  p9  paragraph  [p9-l32]  Analogously to Eq. (14), it then follows that:
  180  p9  formula (22)  [p9-l34..p9-l41 (11)]  ∞ ∞ ∞\na∗ = M ai = a0 ⊕ M ai = 1 ⊕ a ⊗ M ai = 1 ⊕ a ⊗ a∗ (22)\ni=0 i=1 i=0
  181  p9  paragraph  [p9-l44]  and, similarly:
  182  p9  formula (23)  [p9-l46..p9-l50 (8)]  ∞ ∞\na∗ = a0 ⊕ M ai = 1 ⊕ M ai ⊗ a = 1 ⊕ a∗ ⊗ a (23)\ni=1 i=0
  183  p9  paragraph  [p9-l53..p9-l54 (2)]  We now discuss how complete semirings can be lifted to square matrices. The definitions follow\nanalogously to matrices over the real numbers.
  184  p9  paragraph  [p9-l55..p9-l57 (3)]  Definition 13. We define semiring matrix addition as follows. Let A and B be d × d matrices whose\nentries are elements from a complete semiring W = ⟨A, ⊕, ⊗, 0, 1⟩. Then the sum ("+") of A and B is\ndefined as:
  185  p9  formula (24)  [p9-l58..p9-l61 (4)]  (A + B)ij def= Aij ⊕ Bij i, j ∈ 1, . . . , d (24)
  186  p10  paragraph  [p10-l1..p10-l3 (3)]  Definition 14. We define semiring matrix multiplication as follows. Let A and B be d × d matrices\nwhose entries are elements from a complete semiring W = ⟨A, ⊕, ⊗, 0, 1⟩. Then the product of A and B\nis defined as:
  187  p9  furniture/page-number  [p9-l62]  65
  188  p10  formula (25)  [p10-l6..p10-l7 (7)]  d\n(AB)ij def= M Aik ⊗ Bkj i, j ∈ 1, . . . , d (25)\nk=1
  189  p10  paragraph  [p10-l11..p10-l16 (6)]  We also define the zero matrix, O, over the complete semiring W = ⟨A, ⊕, ⊗, 0, 1⟩, such that all\nentries are 0, and the unit matrix I as (I)ij = 1 iff i = j and 0 otherwise for all indices i, j ∈ 0, . . . , d.\nIt is then straightforward to show that matrix addition is associative and commutative, while matrix\nmultiplication is associative and distributive over matrix addition. Hence, the set of square matrices\nof dimension d over a semiring, with addition and multiplication as defined above, is itself a semiring.\nFurthermore, by the element-wise definition of its addition operation, it is also complete.
  190  p10  heading (B.2)  [p10-l17..p10-l18 (2)]  B.2 Semiring-weighted prefix algorithm
  191  p10  paragraph  [p10-l19..p10-l25 (7)]  We now consider a semiring-weighted CFG G = ⟨N, Σ, S, R, p, W⟩, where N, Σ, S, R are defined as\nbefore but the weighting function p: R → W now maps rules to elements of a commutative semiring\nW.9 Note that we no longer require the rule weights to sum to one. As before, we define the matrix P\nas the square matrix of dimension |N| whose rows and columns are indexed by the non-terminals N in\nsome fixed order so that the entry Pij corresponds to the weight of getting the non-terminal Xj on the left\nafter one rule application to Xi. Since the rule weights are no longer locally normalized, however, we\nneed to include the treesum under the right non-terminal of each rule as an additional term:10
  192  p10  formula (26)  [p10-l26..p10-l28 (5)]  Pij = p(Xi −→ Xj _) def= M p(Xi −→ XjY) · treesum(Y) (26)\nY∈N
  193  p10  paragraph  [p10-l31..p10-d2 (7)]  {p10-d1..p10-d2 (2)@213}  We can then calculate the weight of getting Xj from Xi at the leftmost non-terminal after exactly k\nderivation steps as (Pk)ij, where Pk def= P · · · P. Finally, to get the left-corner expectations, we then\n| {z }
  194  p10  paragraph  [p10-l35]  k times
  195  p10  paragraph  [p10-l37..p10-l42 (6)]  need to calculate the Kleene closure over the matrix P,11 that is, we want to find P∗ = P∞k=0 Pk. To\ncompute the Kleene closure over the transition matrix we can use an efficient algorithm by Lehmann\n(1977) which is a generalization of the well-known shortest-path algorithm usually attributed to Floyd\n(1962) and Warshall (1962), but introduced previously by Roy (1959).12 The algorithm works under the\ncondition that the Kleene closure of all individual matrix entries from semiring W exists, which is true for\nour case since we assumed W to be complete. The algorithm is shown in Alg. 4.
  196  p10  caption (Algorithm 4) -> 197  [p10-l43]  Algorithm 4 Lehmann’s algorithm for computing the Kleene closure over a transition matrix
  197  p10  figure  [p10-d3..p10-d6 (29)]  {p10-d3..p10-d4 (2)@0 p10-d5..p10-d6 (2)@251}  1: def Lehmann(M):\n2: d ←− dim(M) ▷ M is a d × d matrix over a complete semiring\n3: M(0) ←− M\n4: for j = 1, . . . , d :\n5: for i = 1, . . . , d :\n6: for k = 1, . . . , d :\n7: M(j)ik ←− M(j−1) ik ⊕ M(j−1) ij ⊗ M(j−1) jj ∗ ⊗ M(j−1) jk\n8: return I + M(d)
  198  p10  footnote (9)  [p10-l69]  9We require that W be commutative because the order of rule applications does not affect string weight in a weighted CFG.
  199  p10  footnote (10)  [p10-l70..p10-l72 (3)]  10For locally normalized semirings, the treesum of any non-terminal is 1. In the general case when the weights are not\nnormalized, the treesum of a semiring-weighted WCFG can be computed through fixed point iteration in a similar way to\nNewton’s method (Esparza et al., 2007).
  200  p10  footnote (11)  [p10-l73]  11Note that the Kleene closure exists since matrices with elements from a complete semiring are complete.
  201  p10  footnote (12)  [p10-l74..p10-l76 (3)]  12The generalization is by way of choosing the appropriate semiring for the given problem. By the same token, Lehmann’s\nalgorithm can also be seen as a generalization of Kleene’s (1956) algorithm for converting finite-state automata to regular\nexpressions and the Gauss–Jordan algorithm for computing matrix inversion (see e.g. Althoen and McLaughlin (1987)).
  202  p10  furniture/page-number  [p10-l77]  66
  203  p11  paragraph  [p11-l1..p11-l3 (3)]  Lastly, since we no longer have normalized rule weights, we need to set the prefix weight of ε under\nany non-terminal X ∈ N to the treesum under X. With this, we can now generalize our prefix weight\nalgorithm to semirings, as shown in Alg. 5.
  204  p11  caption (Algorithm 5) -> 205  [p11-l4]  Algorithm 5 Faster prefix algorithm over semirings
  205  p11  figure  [p11-d1..p11-d3 (66)]  {p11-d1..p11-d2 (2)@0 p11-d3@829}  1: P′ ←− Lehmann(P) ▷ Precompute P∗ with Alg. 4\n2: for Xi, Xj ∈ N : ▷ Assign ξ(Xj | Xi)\n3: ξ(Xj | Xi) ←− P′ij\n4: def FastSemiringJL(w = w1 · · · wN, G):\n5: π(·, ·, ·) ←− 0 ▷ Initialize prefix probabilities\n6: for k ∈ 0, . . . , N :\n7: for X ∈ N : ▷ Prefix weight of ε\n8: π(k, X, k) ←− treesum(X)\n9: β ←− CKY(w) ▷ Compute β with Alg. 1\n10: for i, j = 0, . . . , N :\n11: for X, Z ∈ N : ▷ Compute γ by Eq. (16e)\n12: γij(X, Z)←− L p(X−→YZ) ⊗ β(i, Y, j)\nY∈N\n13: for X, Z ∈ N : ▷ Compute δ by Eq. (16f)\n14: δij(X, Z)←− L ξ(Y | X) ⊗ γij(Y, Z)\nY∈N\n15: for k ∈ 0, . . . , N − 1 :\n16: for X ∈ N : ▷ Compute base case\n17: π(k, X, k+1)←− L ξ(Y | X) ⊗ p(Y−→wk+1)\nY∈N\n18: for ℓ ∈ 2 . . . N :\n19: for i ∈ 1 . . . N − ℓ :\n20: k ←− i + ℓ\n21: for X, Z ∈ N : ▷ Recursively compute π\nk−1\n22: π(i, X, k) += L δij(X, Z) ⊗ π(j, Z, k)\nj=i+1\n23: return π
  206  p11  paragraph  [p11-l68]  Proposition 4. The semiring-weighted version of our algorithm runs in O(N2|N|3 + N3|N|2).
  207  p11  paragraph  [p11-l69..p11-l75 (7)]  Proof. Lehmann’s algorithm, as presented in Alg. 4, has three nested for loops of d iterations each, where\nd is the dimension of the input matrix. In our case, d is the number of non-terminals, |N|. Assuming the\nKleene closure of elements in W can be evaluated in O(1), this means that computing the left corner\nexpectations in line 1 of Alg. 5 takes O(|N|3), as before. Hence, the complexity of the overall algorithm\nremains unchanged, that is, we can compute the prefix probabilities under a semiring-weighted, locally\nnormalized CFG G in O(N2|N|3 + N3|N|2). ■
  208  p11  furniture/page-number  [p11-l76]  67
