  122  p5  paragraph  [p5-l134..p6-l21 (22)]  [59] The limit of ∆ → 0 is related to the fact that the integral\nof a limit and the limit of an integral are generally not\nthe same. This behaviour is also present in the Berry\ncurvature, as discussed in Ref. [81] for anomalous Hall\neffect. In this paper, if we take the ∆ → 0 at the very\nbeginning of graphene Hamiltonian, the OHE disappears\naltogether. But if we take the ∆ → 0 limit for the orbital\nmoment, it yields lim∆→0∆/(q2v2F + ∆2) = πδ(q2v2F).\nFor the intrinsic case, the Fermi energy lies exactly at the\nDirac point. The ∆ → 0 limit will make the intrinsic OH-\nconductance diverge after performing the integral over\nthe Fermi sea [33]. Hence, formally, the intrinsic OH-\nconductance will diverge when the EF is in band gap and\nthe extrinsic OH-conductance will vanish for graphene.\nHowever, in realistic samples, we expect that once the\ngap reaches below a certain value it will be overwhelmed\nby disorder and such a divergence will not be observable\nin practice. The limit of vanishing gap makes sense only\nfor graphene which is a gapless material in a pristine\nsituation. The ∆ → 0 limit is not applicable to TMDs,\nsince the band gap of monolayer TMDs is inherent to the\nband structure of materials [45].
